diff --git a/spec/QUOTA.md b/spec/QUOTA.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..56a859d2
--- /dev/null
+++ b/spec/QUOTA.md
@@ -0,0 +1,136 @@
+# Codex quota contract (macOS phase one)
+
+This is a local, account-level quota observation, distinct from token usage, spend, and the remote collector's metrics. The Codex provider reads the locally available Codex session and queries the account's usage endpoint. Do not transmit credentials or quota data to the collector, include credentials in logs, or write credentials back. The endpoint and fields below are observed provider behavior, not a guarantee of a stable OpenAI API.
+
+On macOS, the provider reads `auth.json` from a non-empty `CODEX_HOME` directory when set. Otherwise, it reads `~/.codex/auth.json`. The file is read-only; credentials are used only for the local account request. The provider follows the same snapshot, endpoint, status, freshness, and backoff semantics described below.
+
+## Snapshot
+
+A snapshot has the following logical fields. Optional values are nullable; a missing reading is **unknown**, not zero. Dates are instants (when serialized, ISO 8601 UTC); durations are seconds. IDs are stable machine identifiers, not localized presentation strings.
+
+| Field | Meaning |
+| --- | --- |
+| `id` | Provider identifier, `codex` for this provider. |
+| `displayName` | Human-readable provider name. |
+| `fidelity` | One of `official`, `derived`, or `manual`: respectively a provider's own endpoint, an inferred reading, or a user-entered reading. Codex uses `official`, which does not imply an externally guaranteed API. |
+| `status` | One of `ok`, `stale`, `needsAuth`, `accessDenied`, `unsupported`, `error`, defined below. |
+| `windows` | Zero or more individually identified quota windows. |
+| `headlineId` | Nullable ID of the designated headline window; consumers resolve by ID, not array position. It must not select an unrelated extra just because the primary is absent. |
+| `block` | Nullable explanatory reason that usage is blocked, when known. Do not invent one from missing data. |
+| `account`, `plan` | Nullable account and subscription plan labels, respectively; they are not credentials. |
+| `observedAt` | Nullable time of the last successful observation, not the time an old observation was displayed. |
+| `source` | Description/identifier of the source of the observation. |
+| `nextAllowedAt` | Nullable earliest next permitted refresh time, if the provider is backing off. |
+| `configured` | Nullable boolean: `false` means the source machine identified no credential configuration for this provider (no session file, key, environment token or collector credential). An absent value keeps legacy behaviour. |
+| `waitingForRefresh` | True only when a persisted successful polling interval prevents a refresh and no observation is available in this process. Status remains `error` for compatibility; display this condition as waiting, not a failed request. |
+| `credits` | Nullable credit information: `hasCredits` and `unlimited` are nullable booleans, `balance` is a nullable decimal string. `limit` and `used` are nullable numeric absolute amounts in provider-reported spend units, with no inferred currency; `usedFraction` and `resetAt` describe the individual spend-control allowance. A balance of `"0"` is known zero, not unknown. The allowance's `usedFraction` is not clamped and may exceed 1. |
+| `resetCreditsAvailable` | Nullable nonnegative count of available rate-limit reset cards, queried from the separate reset-credits endpoint on macOS. Independent of usage credit balance and spend allowance; missing or failed lookups are unknown, never zero. |
+
+A provider is never a permanent slot: the account list includes a source only while its latest snapshot reports identified credential configuration (`configured` is not `false`). A missing, logged-out or uninstalled CLI must not produce a placeholder card; the source keeps polling in the background so a later CLI login can surface it. The source switch list still lists every implemented provider so selection can be prepared in advance. A collector reports `configured` from credentials present on the collector host.
+
+A window contains `id`, `label`, `usedFraction`, `remaining`, `used`, `resetAt`, and `duration`. `id` is stable and unique within a snapshot; `label` is display text. For Codex windows, `usedFraction` and `used` are the reported percentage divided by 100, and `remaining` is 1 minus that fraction. These nullable fractions are not absolute counts and are not clamped: usage may exceed 1 and remaining may be negative. Zero is known zero, not unknown. `resetAt` is a nullable absolute instant and `duration` is a nullable window length in seconds. Unknown fields remain null, including an unknown fraction on an otherwise valid window.
+
+`primary_window` and `secondary_window` are source slots, not synonyms for five-hour and weekly windows: they can be swapped, have equal or unknown durations, or one can be missing. Identify a known-duration window by its source family and duration, adding a slot suffix for same-family duration collisions; use an `unknown:slot` fallback when duration is unknown. Namespace feature-scoped extras and code review separately from the main windows and one another. Never assume a particular duration from a primary or secondary slot.
+
+## Status and freshness
+
+* `ok`: a usable fresh observation was obtained.
+* `stale`: a previous successful observation is being displayed after a later refresh failed or became unavailable; preserve its original `observedAt` and do not present it as fresh.
+* `needsAuth`: no usable local session, or authentication is rejected.
+* `accessDenied`: the account is authenticated but access is explicitly denied; do not conflate absence of quota with a confirmed denial.
+* `unsupported`: the provider/account or response is known not to support this quota surface, rather than merely returning missing fields.
+* `error`: a network, decoding, service, or other failure that does not establish a more specific state.
+
+An authentication failure must not be represented as zero usage. If old data is retained, it must be visibly stale; a previous timestamp must never be advanced on an unsuccessful refresh. Rate limiting is a transient failure, not an empty successful observation. Respect server retry guidance and avoid repeatedly querying the endpoint while it is rate-limited.
+macOS persists the last successfully parsed, structured account snapshot and hydrate it at application startup, including with networking disabled. Restored observations retain their original `observedAt`, unknown values and reported reset instants, and are visibly stale until a new successful response; an elapsed reset never synthesizes a full allowance. Cache data is private, versioned and partitioned by the current authenticated account's digest, with no tokens, request headers or raw responses. Missing, corrupt, unsupported-version or mismatched-account cache entries are cache misses, not zero readings. An absent or rejected local identity must not expose another account's cached observation. Actual 429, decoding, network and service failures remain errors or stale observations, never successful waiting states. Legacy schedules without a usable observation may still produce `waitingForRefresh` until their preserved deadline.
+
+## Desktop presentation
+
+Along the edge, use each account’s own measured ring-and-reading extent with eight-unit outer padding and eight-unit inter-account gaps; a single-window or unknown account never reserves another account’s taller multi-window cell. Keep the four-unit ring-to-reading gap, existing font sizes and row spacing, and all reading rows. On left/right edges the body depth is the larger of 70 logical units and the widest account content plus four-unit side insets; top/bottom depth and along-edge padding remain unchanged. Each account’s width is the larger of its unchanged outer ring diameter and its actual native-font reading width. Paint, hit rectangles, tooltip targets, native window extents and placement consume this same content layout on all four edges.
+
+Measure complete, unwrapped reading strings with the same native font used to render them: AppKit NSFont on macOS and WPF FormattedText on Windows. Multi-window rows share the widest period/scope label and value columns within their account, with a five-unit period dot, four-unit dot-to-label gap and six-unit label-to-value gap. Do not reserve a fixed 90-unit reading width, cap the value column, round away percentage precision, shrink text, truncate long scopes/durations, or force values such as `92.53%` or `96.04%` to wrap to hit a width target. Unknown, authentication and over-limit readings use the same measurement policy; long content may legitimately widen the panel. No additional native measurement work belongs in animation frames.
+
+Quota is presented in the existing Settings window as source-neutral account cards. Every actual window is visible on Settings, floating bodies, floating details and any tray/menu summary; `headlineId` remains metadata, not permission to hide other windows. Sort known durations from longest to shortest, with stable identifiers breaking ties and preserving distinct feature scopes. Labels and durations identify windows, never provider response slots. Display remaining and used together where space permits; do not calculate an aggregate percentage across independent limits.
+
+Subscription is a source-neutral account list and settings group, with the real Codex adapter; do not invent accounts, providers or readings. Account cards do not collapse: use a compact identity/known-plan header, full-width quota windows and one wrapping footer for known credit balance, available reset-card count and the original absolute local update timestamp. Keep spend-control allowances explicitly labelled and separate. Missing optional values do not create repeated unknown labels, fictional quota windows or measured-zero tracks. A provider without an observation gets one concise state-specific explanation. Known zero and retained stale readings remain visible, with original observation time and stale status. Source provenance, fidelity and next-permitted-request timestamps are not account-card content. Preserve Light, Dark and System settings.
+
+macOS Subscription follows the approved account-card layout using native controls: the account list is primary, with a separate refresh toolbar. Remaining percentages use the same readable type size as quota labels. Each known reset shows its absolute local time and the time remaining until that instant; an elapsed reset is pending refresh, not a negative duration. Countdown text updates locally while the page is active without making provider requests or rebuilding the page. Settings use restrained aligned rows and a source editor with All sources, its include-future-sources explanation, and individual source choices. All sources is persisted independently of the explicit selected-source list and disables the individual choices while enabled. The page has neither a diagnostics disclosure nor a credential-storage footer. Actual floating-panel failure messages remain visible.
+
+Available reset cards may include a nearest reported expiration, `resetCreditsNextExpiry`, parsed independently of the count from valid ISO-8601 `expires_at` fields of entries whose status is exactly `available`. Used and expired cards do not supply this value. Missing or invalid dates remain unknown; count, quota-window reset and observation time never substitute for expiration. A partial list establishes only the nearest known reported expiration. Do not show a card expiration when the authoritative available count is zero.
+
+Quota graphics show remaining allowance; clamp only graphic fill, not reported numeric labels or the underlying used fraction. Unknown readings remain unknown. Window colors identify periods, not warning severity: monthly blue, weekly green, five-hour lilac; warnings use separate status text. Persisted source and automatic-refresh preferences control providers independently, and manual refresh still obeys their backoff.
+
+On macOS the floating details card's bottom successful-observation label is a short relative update age derived from the original `observedAt`, not a timeless “latest” indicator: “Updated just now”, “Updated Xm ago”, “Updated Xh ago” or “Updated Xd ago” (Simplified Chinese: “刚刚更新”, “X分钟前更新”, “X小时前更新”, “X天前更新”; Traditional Chinese and Japanese are localized too). Missing `observedAt` is unknown and future observations never produce negative ages. Offline, stale, waiting, authentication and error states keep precedence. The details card and an open tray/menu summary refresh only their visible local labels at the next observation-relative minute boundary: opening an observation aged 59 or 119 seconds schedules one second, not another full minute. Recompute the deadline after snapshot/visibility changes, clamp sub-millisecond deadlines to one millisecond, and stop on hidden/unloaded/disposed displays. The collapsed/main quota window has no age timer. These clocks never request HTTP or alter provider polling/backoff; Subscription's absolute update timestamp and reset-countdown layout remain unchanged.
+
+Quota startup on macOS belongs to the application, not any visible surface. With a supported source enabled, hydrate the current account's last successful observation, including when network access is disabled. Independently attempt one startup refresh as soon as networking is permitted, even in manual periodic-refresh mode and without opening Settings or the floating panel. This one-shot startup request may bypass only the ordinary five-minute floor following a successful observation; persisted 429 Retry-After, failure backoff and unchanged-token authentication rejection still apply. Source and network toggles within the same application lifetime do not repeatedly grant this exception. Automatic mode then continues the normal five-minute cadence while all surfaces are closed; manual mode stops periodic requests. Explicit non-startup refreshes still obey the ordinary floor and backoff. Network errors retain the last good snapshot with its original observation time. Cancellation, disposal and account/token changes must prevent obsolete in-flight results from replacing the currently displayed account or its persisted snapshot.
+
+Automatic polling schedules per-source retries when a refresh returns a transiently unavailable observation (`error` or `stale`), rather than waiting for the next ordinary five-minute tick. Cold start means the current account/source has no validated successful observation; an available account-bound cached observation is displayed stale immediately and is not cold. Before the first successful observation, genuine transport, service and decoding failures retry after 5, 10, 20 and then at most 30 seconds per attempt. Both the native scheduler and the credential-owning provider enforce this short cadence: a five-second UI wake blocked by a hidden sixty-second provider floor does not satisfy the contract. Remote connection readiness triggers the existing immediate selected-source startup read. The first successful observation clears cold-start pacing and restores normal polling; failures with an already available observation retain the ordinary backoff policy.
+
+A provider's future `nextAllowedAt` is authoritative. HTTP 429 retains its conservative supplier rate-limit protection even without a valid header, and an explicit `Retry-After` on any transient response (including a 503 or OpenCode Console probe) cannot be shortened to the cold-start cap. Optional Codex reset-credit throttling remains binding without invalidating successfully observed usage. Persisted deadlines survive restarts; a legacy future deadline of unknown cause is not blindly shortened because it may be a rate limit. After that deadline, new results use the explicit cold/server pacing rules. Cache hydration and blocked requests do not count as additional HTTP failures. Expired deadlines cannot produce a tight loop. Authentication, permission and unsupported states do not enter rapid retry.
+
+Retries wake only the affected source and coalesce with in-flight work; no synthetic zero reading is created. Turning automatic refresh off, revoking network access, removing a source or disposing the application cancels its pending retry work, and stale callbacks/results cannot re-arm it. Existing manual/startup preference semantics remain unchanged. These rules apply to Codex, OpenCode Go and Kimi on both native clients and the shared remote collector; no extra SSH process or autonomous collector quota polling is introduced.
+For migration, a legacy schedule without a validated version-1 account snapshot cannot distinguish a normal successful floor from an optional reset-credit 429 extension. Preserve its future `nextAllowedAt` rather than granting the startup bypass. After that deadline, a successful request upgrades the structured snapshot and independent retry deadline; subsequent application starts may bypass only the ordinary successful floor.
+
+Both floating panels retain full-strength provider logos, allowance rings and numerical readings when showing an old observation, including after a refresh failure, with network access disabled or when the observation is more than fifteen minutes old. Stale status, the original observation time and details remain visible; full brightness never marks old data as fresh. Ring background tracks, unknown-reading styling and the normal expansion/collapse fade are independent and unchanged.
+
+By default, without pointer attention, the macOS panel start and rest as a collapsed strip: 10 by 79 logical pixels on vertical edges or 79 by 10 on horizontal edges, solid black in both themes, with no icon, value, settings control or grip. Only that visible strip accepts input; the rest of the fixed canvas remains available to the desktop. Pointer entry expands the body with a finite 220 ms eased transition; leaving the body or details card retains the existing 250 ms grace period before collapse. Moving from body to card must not collapse or flash the body. The body stays expanded while the pointer is in either window, a context menu is open, a drag is pending or active, or the grace period is running. The existing reduced-motion preference settles transitions immediately. Expansion and collapse retain the fixed canvas and body-center anchor on all four edges and at every DPI; resting collapsed or expanded states do not keep a rendering subscription alive. Expanded contents continue to follow the resolved light/dark theme.
+
+On macOS the Subscription preference “Keep expanded” (Simplified Chinese: “保持展开”) sits immediately after the floating-panel visibility switch. It defaults off and persists independently of visibility and monitor/edge/along placement; missing or nonboolean values retain the off default without a settings-version migration. When enabled, the first visible body is already expanded at startup or re-show, even if Settings has never been opened. Leaving the panel does not collapse its body. This preference changes only the body target: it does not synthesize pointer attention, reveal the six-dot grip, open an account card, refresh quota or rebuild a provider. The resting arc still follows body expansion and the arc/disc/dots retain their independent real hover/held interaction. Disabling the preference applies immediately but preserves any real body/card/menu/grace/pending-drag/active-drag attention until that interaction ends. Hide/close clear interaction and stop finite motion; re-show reads the preference again, and settled pinned rest has no continuous rendering subscription.
+
+The expanded macOS floating panel have an independently visible quarter-circle affordance in the trailing flare pocket. This is not the body's outline, a blank body hotspot, an internal footer or a settings shortcut. Settings remains available through the context menu and tray/main Settings entry; no gear or permanent disc is restored. The original Codenotch `SettingsHandle.swift` describes the arc as saying "there is something here". The reference Windows geometry (`windows/codenotch/ui/notch.html` at `835550a`, lines 1983–1984 and 2223–2354) supplies GooArc, DiscToArc and DiscMerge shapes. Arc and temporary disc retain their 0.9 reference scale: arc radius 25.65 logical pixels, fill stroke 6.12 with an 8.12 outer stroke, and disc radius 20.97. The enlarged dot grid is independently 8.0 neutral diameter and 12.5 pitch, with the existing 30% hover growth to 10.4, one-pixel outlines and held squeeze ratios unchanged. Do not globally enlarge the arc or body to enlarge the dots. All are derived from the flare center `(38.7, BodyEnd + 38.7)`; the final grid moves only eight logical pixels along the edge from that center rather than adding the original 43.2 external reach. Left/right grids are three columns by two rows; the same edge transform makes top/bottom grids two columns by three rows. Fill and outlines use the panel palette (dark `#000000`/`#2e2e2e`, light `#f5f5f7`/`#d2d2d7`), not six bright white ink dots.
+
+The outlined resting arc has 8.49 DIP of true radial clearance from the outlined circular body flare; the temporary full disc has 17.23 DIP. Neutral outlined dot paint spans X 21.7–55.7 and Y `BodyEnd + 35.95`–`BodyEnd + 57.45`, a 34-by-21.5-DIP envelope; settled hover paint is 36.4 by 23.9 DIP. Hit padding, radial bridge, activation fan and liquid core/neck consumers use the actual production dot/grid geometry, not independently enlarged SVGs or a blanket input rectangle. `GripPaintClearance` measures the actual quarter curve including its endpoints, not the full circle or a bounding rectangle; `ArcGripPaintClearance` measures the retained outlined arc against all six outlined dots, including hover. These production methods drive regressions and the frame probe. The animation canvas still conservatively reserves 32.97 DIP beyond the flare for transient disc/filter bounds, without making that envelope interactive or moving the screen anchor.
+
+Expansion alone starts the independent arc separation (340-ms delay, 950-ms cubic-bezier `(0.35,0,0.25,1)`), so the resting expanded body presents a visible entry without first finding an invisible hotspot. Hovering the control family rolls the arc toward a temporary source disc over 220 ms; after a further 20 ms, the disc contracts into the nearby six drops over the reference 420-ms division curve. The original entry arc remains visibly painted and hittable throughout that morph and alongside the final dots. The disc is not another permanently lit black ball. On leave, drops flow back over 200 ms, the disc waits another 100 ms and rolls back into the arc over 400 ms. Collapse uses the reference 260-ms quick arc return. Hover uses a 220-ms-response/0.55-damping spring; a captured press squeezes dot radius by 30% and pitch by 18% without shifting its painted center. Reduced motion settles every channel and finite terminal states unsubscribe from rendering.
+
+Each frame derives the visible entry arc, temporary disc, core/neck, six drops and their input routes from the same `GripFrame`. The moving small control is the current six-dot paint plus two-DIP padding (28.31 by 19.4 DIP at its normal terminal pose). An eight-DIP radial bridge crosses only the actual body-to-arc gap; while the family is active it continues from the entry toward the pocket center, joining the short center-to-dots corridor. These narrow routes are intentionally alpha 1/255; the visible arc itself never disappears from hit classification during a morph. There is no invisible disc-sized or envelope-sized hit rectangle. Arbitrarily slow movement between body, arc, disc, routes and dots must keep the whole family expanded and revealed; merely visiting the quota card must not light six dots over desktop text. Grip-family and Alt-body presses capture before the same four-DIP radial threshold in all directions; pending/active gestures hold expansion and release resamples and persists the physical monitor/edge/along anchor. Quota cells, details and refresh semantics remain separate from drag handling.
+
+While the disc or drops are active, `GetControlFanPath` adds only the eight-DIP straight corridors from the resting arc's two endpoints and midpoint to each current drop center. This supports direct, arbitrarily slow travel to any of the six dots rather than requiring an undiscoverable centerline detour. Coincident early drops share a corridor. The compound path uses nonzero winding so route overlaps union without holes; it stays within the arc-to-dot pocket plus the four-DIP corridor half-width, is painted at alpha 1/255, and is absent from ordinary expanded arc-only rest. The fixed animation envelope and unused parts of the orb-sized pocket are not promoted to input rectangles.
+
+Pointer entry during the arc's initial emergence also retains its exact last-painted GooArc phase and return flags in `GripFrame`. `CreateOrbCarry` reconstructs that same visible source and filtered input while the resting entry and disc/drop transition proceed; the carry clears only after the entire family returns. This prevents a stationary pointer on the moving, compressed arc from falling through when the source changes. It stores scalar motion state, not a rendered snapshot, rectangle or continuously sampled pointer history.
+
+Subscription Settings no longer exposes runtime diagnostics or a credential-storage footnote. Safe initialization/placement stage and exception type/HResult state may remain internal to the native host for tests; no page callback or hidden replacement diagnostic UI is retained. Pointer events and finite transitions drive the panel lifecycle; it has no continuous cursor, browser bridge, or bitmap polling loop. Provider refresh and backoff remain independent of this rendering lifecycle.
+
+The optional edge-attached floating renderer retains the matte central supplier-logo disk, round progress caps and concave native flares from [Codenotch at commit `835550a342871779135b6484d6899161c5222f7c`](https://github.com/vinzdg/codenotch/tree/835550a342871779135b6484d6899161c5222f7c). Each account has its own ring group. Periods run longest to shortest from outside inward: month, week, five-hour. Missing windows reserve no empty ring. Additional windows remain represented with stable labels, not silently discarded. The center logo/disk does not shrink as rings are added; the actual body, stable canvas, placement and native input geometry expand together to contain the outer ring and every period reading outside the flares. A genuine zero retains its track; unknown is distinguishable from zero. No synthetic provider or quota reading is presented as real.
+
+The existing transient menu-bar popover or tray flyout shows a compact summary of every selected account, including healthy ones, and every available quota window within each account. Authentication, block, low-allowance, unknown and offline states stay explicit; there is no account-count cap. With networking disabled, previously hydrated snapshots remain visible with the offline status instead of disappearing. Open the existing Settings quota page for details.
+
+Claims of pixel-identical rendering require comparison of native screenshots against the pinned Codenotch reference at the same DPI, presentation state, and account data. No current native screenshot comparison proves pixel identity.
+
+Ordinary quota regressions must not pin historical pointer coordinates, default panel/card dimensions, ring/logo sizes, outline clearances or literal path fragments. Those are visual layout choices that can legitimately change with provider/window count and typography, not durable release contracts. The obsolete `OldAltDragPointsAreCellContentRatherThanDraggableBody` and related fixed-point body assertions are removed, rather than re-pinned, skipped or satisfied by changing production layout. Keep semantic provider/window data, cancellation/persistence, hover/drag lifecycle, edge attachment and paint-to-input/containment relationships derived from the actual current geometry. Coordinates used as inputs to placement/DPI algorithms remain valid algorithm tests. Optional visual diagnostics are not substitutes for ordinary behavior tests or extra Release gates.
+
+The macOS renderer uses finite scalar motion tracks and a closed-form damped spring for the independent body, arc, disc, reveal, hover and press states. Delayed tracks retain the exact previously painted pose until their start boundary, matching Windows. Reduced motion settles immediately, and a terminal state stops the frame subscription. Geometry, visible paint, native hit testing and the local liquid mask share the same frame; none is a browser preview or a desktop-wide input rectangle.
+
+Native rendering diagnostics are optional developer tooling, separate from ordinary `swift test` and the Release test gate. From the prepared tree root on macOS, run:
+
+```sh
+swift test --package-path macos --filter QuotaNotchTests
+swift run --package-path macos --configuration debug AgentSpeed --quota-notch-visual-checks /tmp/quota-notch-native
+```
+
+The first command runs the ordinary geometry, state, motion, hit-routing, folding, unknown/status, and reset-formatting regressions; it does not render offscreen images or compare pixels/PNG encodings. The second explicitly opts into `QuotaNotchVisualHarness` via the existing debug launch-option mechanism. It renders the actual Codex `QuotaNotchView` and painted control-family input mask on four edges, in both themes at 1x and 2x, for twelve body/control/status states (192 notch PNGs), plus four localized details PNGs. It exits nonzero on diagnostic failure, including missing native images or PNG encoding/write failures. This opt-in native route is not part of ordinary test discovery or the Release gate. It runs before application activation without real credentials and requires the native macOS SDK and a GUI login session.
+
+The optional captures use fixed synthetic frames and credential-free observations, not live requests or substitute browser graphics. They exercise folded, resting arc, emerging arc, temporary disc, dividing drops, six drops, held, stale, offline, aged, unknown and authentication states. Stale, offline and aged renders are compared with matching fresh native controls so the logo, rings and readings retain full brightness without pinning layout coordinates. The harness also checks tight native bounds, opaque paint against the shared input mask, the resting arc's transparent hole and every revealed drop's draggable target. Its four localized details captures cover English, Simplified Chinese, Traditional Chinese and Japanese.
+
+On macOS the AppKit interception gate and SwiftUI control must share the actual painted control-family target: resting arc, temporary disc, revealed drops and their narrow connecting routes. The resting arc's center hole remains transparent. A settings button or stale circular wake region is not a substitute for the grip, and hovering a visible moving arc must not make its last-painted target disappear. Tucked controls are not clickable. Native tests cover all edges, control-family transitions, hosting-view hit routing, and transparent unused canvas; Linux syntax checks do not establish these native input properties.
+
+The optional visual harness also compares the native ring rendering against an independent circular reference with the same period-colored progress-over-track compositing. Native color-space and rasterization differences are not evidence of pixel identity to the reference application. These native checks remain unverified until run on macOS; portable motion tests and syntax parsing do not replace AppKit/SwiftUI SDK type checking, native paint/input acceptance, font measurements or Retina capture.
+
+
+Reset formatting tests compare the localized wrapper around independently formatted calendar dates, rather than banning punctuation from the entire string. Below seven calendar days the payload includes weekday and localized time (`E j:mm`); at seven days and beyond it contains month and day (`MMM d`). The supplied calendar's time zone is respected explicitly. Tests pin a Gregorian UTC calendar and cover the minute-to-date transition and both sides of the seven-day boundary without pinning the localized wording.
+
+Unknown usage and reset values are shown as `—` or unknown, never as zero or an invented reset. A known zero remains zero. Show non-official fidelity with `~`; status takes precedence over numerical readings. Retained readings must be visibly stale, with their original observation time or age, and must not appear fresh. Show authentication, access, unsupported, waiting, and error states distinctly rather than implying a successful reading. Display a known block separately from window usage, including its reason when available; do not infer a block from missing data or conflate it with a percentage. Progress graphics may be bounded visually, but labels must preserve reported over-limit usage and negative remaining values.
+
+## Codex usage endpoint
+
+`GET https://chatgpt.com/backend-api/wham/usage` uses the local Codex session's bearer access token and `ChatGPT-Account-Id`; `Accept: application/json` requests JSON. Never place either credential in fixtures or logs. `plan_type` is the plan when supplied. The main `rate_limit.primary_window` and `rate_limit.secondary_window` are independent; parse each without discarding a valid sibling because another is null or malformed. A window's numeric `used_percent` converts to `usedFraction` by dividing by 100 without clamping. `limit_window_seconds` gives duration. `reset_at` is Unix epoch seconds; when absent, `reset_after_seconds` is relative to the observation time. Null or omitted data stays unknown. A non-null 0 percentage is a valid measurement.
+`additional_rate_limits` is an array of feature-scoped rate-limit objects (`limit_name`, `metered_feature`, `rate_limit`), not a replacement for the main account windows. `code_review_rate_limit` may contain another pair. Preserve source scope when representing these windows. A malformed extra must not destroy usable main data. `spend_control.individual_limit` is a separate credit-backed allowance: its `limit` and `used` can be decimal strings, `used_percent` is a percentage, and `reset_at` is epoch seconds. The response's `credits` balance is distinct from that allowance and from unused reset credits at the separate `/wham/rate-limit-reset-credits` endpoint; none is interchangeable with another.
+
+On macOS, each successful usage poll also queries `GET https://chatgpt.com/backend-api/wham/rate-limit-reset-credits` with the same account credentials. This optional reading never replaces or invalidates usable quota windows. A nonnegative integer `available_count` is authoritative; otherwise only a complete, recognized reset-credit status list can establish the available count. Malformed, unsupported or unavailable responses produce an unknown count, not an invented zero or a retained stale count labelled fresh. A reset-endpoint 429 extends the shared persisted request floor using Retry-After; rejected credentials suppress that optional endpoint for the unchanged token without changing a successful usage observation to an authentication failure. Both requests remain within the provider's coalesced, rate-limited refresh operation; no credentials or account identifiers are included in diagnostics.
+
+Synthetic, credential-free examples: [`fixtures/codex-quota.json`](fixtures/codex-quota.json) shows main windows, an extra, a code-review window with unknown usage, and spend control; [`fixtures/codex-credit-quota.json`](fixtures/codex-credit-quota.json) shows missing rolling windows and a known zero balance. Their timestamps are examples, not current reset predictions. These are illustrative endpoint payloads, not captures of a real account.
+Both endpoint fixtures are raw provider responses, not snapshots; parsing either produces snapshot fidelity `official`.
+
